<Site title="Understanding rainfall departure"><PageHeader title="What is “below normal” rain?" eyebrow="Explainer · data language" description="A percentage departure compares observed millimetres with a specified normal. It is a weather comparison, not a direct measure of usable supply." crumbs={[{label:'Understand',href:'/understand/'},{label:'Rainfall departure'}]} /><div class="shell page-grid"><article><h2>Two numbers, different jobs</h2><p><strong>Millimetres</strong> describe the rain measured over a period. <strong>Percentage departure</strong> compares that total with a reference normal for the same place and window. The normal's years and source matter. When the expected amount is very small, a percentage can look dramatic while the absolute difference is small.</p><div class="figure"><p class="eyebrow">Method, not a 2026 measurement</p><p>Departure (%) = 100 × (observed rain − normal rain) ÷ normal rain.</p><p class="small muted">A zero or missing normal makes this ratio undefined. Neither value should be silently replaced with zero.</p></div><h2>What else to inspect</h2><p>Check the dates, whether the region is a district, state or meteorological subdivision, and whether a short intense event obscures a longer dry spell. Forecast categories and observed departures are different products.</p><h2>What it cannot establish</h2><p>A rain deficit does not itself demonstrate a formal drought notification, crop failure, safe or unsafe water, or a household service interruption. Those require their own evidence.</p></article><aside><p class="eyebrow">Source and method</p><a href={url('/sources/imd_current/')}>IMD rainfall source lead</a><a href={url('/methodology/')}>Comparison rules</a><a href={url('/data-status/')}>Current coverage</a></aside></div></Site>
